    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d215">

                <pb facs="0333" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg253"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Locarno)/576</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Biddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 139</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-17">March 17,
                        1936</date>.<lb/>[Received April 7.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my despatch
                    No. 133 of March 10, 1936,<anchor n="6" xml:id="d215-f1"/> in which the
                    Norwegian views regarding the action of Germany in establishing a military
                    occupancy of the Rhineland were set forth. In relation to the statements made on
                    the second page of this communication concerning Scandinavian joint
                    representation, through Denmark, in the forthcoming meeting of the Council of
                    the League of Nations, it seems of interest to record the stated refusal of Dr.
                    Munch, Foreign Minister of Denmark, to commit the Northern States to a policy of
                    sanctions in consequence of Germany’s alleged breach of the Locarno Pact and the
                    Versailles Treaty through the dispatch of troops into the demilitarized zone.
                    According to my information, Dr. Munch, acting on behalf of the other
                    Scandinavian Governments, is basing his attitude on the stand taken last year,
                    when, following the German declaration of intention to introduce general
                    military service (March 16, 1935)<anchor n="7" xml:id="d215-f2"/> those States
                    had declared that, not being signatories of the Treaty of Versailles, they would
                    take no action in the matter. It is my understanding, obtained from a
                    well-informed official source, that Dr. Munch’s announced antagonism to
                    sanctions had, in view of his joint representation of the interests of the
                    Northern nations, come as a veritable “bombshell” into the current deliberations
                    at London. It would, accordingly, seem that, coupled with <pb facs="0334"
                        n="254" xml:id="pg254"/> the firm insistence on the part of the British and
                    Belgian representatives at London that there shall be no war, and in view of
                    their known opposition to the imposition of sanctions against Germany, this
                    additional evidence of European lack of sympathy with extreme measures may serve
                    to temper French intransigence.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">A. J. Drexel Biddle, Jr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
